
* Next version (unreleased)

  - New code generator: Tree-IL is compiled into a JavaScript AST which is
    then printed. The output is readable modern JavaScript (const/let,
    function declarations, arrow functions, if statements) instead of nested
    immediately invoked functions.

  - GuileScript now explicitly uses JavaScript data and semantics: lists and
    vectors are arrays, symbols and characters are strings, quoted
    association lists are objects, hash tables are Maps and conditionals use
    JavaScript truthiness.

  - Scheme names are converted to camelCase JavaScript names (log-counter =>
    logCounter, even? => isEven, set-x! => setX). Reserved words are
    renamed and clashing names are a compilation error.

  - Named let loops, do loops and functions calling themselves in tail
    position are compiled into while loops.

  - Fix #f (was null), quoted symbols, string escaping, internal definitions
    (only the first one was kept), case and do.

  - Support optional, rest and keyword arguments and case-lambda. Trailing
    keyword arguments in calls become an options object.

  - ES modules: js-import imports npm packages (or any module) and
    define-public/export export definitions.

  - Method call syntax: (.method obj arg ...) => obj.method(arg, ...).

  - Property access syntax: (.-prop obj) => obj.prop. Properties (and js-ref
    paths) can be assigned with set!.

  - Many more built-in procedures (lists, strings, hash tables...) compiled
    inline. New js-obj, js-typeof, js-instanceof and js-delete!. js-ref and
    js-set! accept property paths.

  - define-module: GuileScript modules (e.g. (app util) in app/util.gs) are
    compiled into ES modules, and importing them (with define-module or
    use-modules, also with #:select or #:prefix) imports the names they
    export. New -d option to compile a file and all the GuileScript modules
    it imports, and -L to add directories where modules are searched.

  - vite-plugin-guilescript: a Vite plugin to use .gs files in Vite
    projects (see examples/vite). New --source-imports option to import
    GuileScript modules by the path of their .gs file, for bundler plugins.

  - js-import #:rename '((name . local-name) ...) to rename imported names.

  - use-modules: macros from any Guile module can be used (e.g. match or
    cut), and so can the procedures GuileScript compiles inline (also with
    #:select or #:prefix). Other procedures from Guile modules are a
    compilation error.

  - Multiple values are arrays: (values a b) => [a, b]. receive,
    let-values, let*-values, define-values and call-with-values are compiled
    into array destructuring.

  - define-record-type (R7RS, SRFI-9) is compiled into a JavaScript class,
    and calls to the record procedures into JavaScript expressions (e.g.
    (point-x p) => p.x).

  - guard (R7RS) is compiled into try/catch. New error-object? and
    error-object-message.

  - js-await: functions using it are compiled into async functions (and it
    is a top-level await at the top level of a module).

  - Every name must be defined, imported, declared with js-global (e.g.
    (js-global document)) or be a standard JavaScript global, so typos are
    compilation errors. Unsupported Guile procedures (e.g. display) are
    compilation errors too.

  - Compilation errors and warnings with source locations. New -o option in
    the guilescript command.

  - Test suite (make check, needs Node.js).

* Version 0.2.0 (Jan 30, 2022)

  - Add initial macro support.

  - Rename log:info, log:warn and log:error to console-log, console-warn and
    consoler-error respectively. Also add console-debug.

  - Removed special syntax to access JavaScript objects and added js-ref,
    js-set!, js-new and js-invoke.

* Version 0.1.0 (Jan 21, 2022)

  - Very primitive JavaScript object method calls.

  - Create 'javascript language which actually decompiles from 'tree-il. So basically we do:
    compile(guilescript) -> 'tree-il -> decompile(tree-il) -> javascript.

  - Split function translation into its own file.

  - Fix ending statement semicolon issues.

  - Return statements should only be allowed inside functions.

* Version 0.0.0 (Jan 17, 2022)

  - Initial release.
